<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cradle of civilization</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cradle of civilization is a place and culture in which one of the earliest civilisations emerged independently, a civilisation being a complex society with a state, social stratification, cities and writing or graphic arts. Scholars once thought civilisation began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a> and spread from there; today they generally recognise six cradles: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caral">Caral</a>–Supe civilisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. All depended on agriculture, except possibly Caral–Supe. The first large cities, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a> and Eridu, rose by the 31st century BCE.</p></div>
